# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The City · web frontend — environment
#
#   cp .env.example .env      then fill in the value marked ← FILL THIS
#
# .env is gitignored. Vite exposes ONLY variables prefixed VITE_ or NEXT_PUBLIC_
# to the app (envPrefix in vite.config.ts); all of them resolve in one place,
# src/framework/config/appConfig.ts. Each entry below says WHAT the variable is
# and WHERE to get its value.
#
# ⚠ EVERYTHING HERE SHIPS TO THE BROWSER. Vite inlines these into the built
#   JavaScript at build time. Nothing here can be a secret. The backend's
#   GEMINI_API_KEY, DB_PASS and BEACON_SECRET must NEVER appear in this file.
#
# ⚠ Vite reads .env at DEV-SERVER START and at BUILD. Restart `npm run dev`
#   after editing; HMR will not pick these up.
#
# ── The project, once, for reference ─────────────────────────────────────────
#   Firebase / GCP project   warroom-498513   (project number 262374983592)
#   Sign-in method           email + password only (signInWithEmailAndPassword)
#   Authorized domains       warroom-498513.firebaseapp.com, warroom-498513.web.app
#   Backend (prod)           https://warroom-academy-backend-262374983592.us-central1.run.app
#   This app (prod)          https://humanfirst-city.vercel.app   (Vercel)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


# ── Firebase sign-in ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The City has no sign-up of its own. Players authenticate against the SAME
# Firebase project as the main WarRoom site; the backend then mirrors their
# WarRoom identity. An account unknown to WarRoom is redirected to register.

# WHAT:  The Firebase *web* ("browser") API key.                   ← FILL THIS
#
# WHERE: three ways, easiest first —
#
#   1. Firebase console → Project settings → General → "Your apps" → the Web
#      app → SDK setup and configuration → `apiKey`.
#
#   2. From the CLI. Take the <UID> of the key named "Browser key (auto created
#      by Firebase)" from the first command, and resolve it with the second:
#        gcloud services api-keys list --project=warroom-498513
#        gcloud services api-keys get-key-string \
#          <UID> --project=warroom-498513
#
#   3. Copy NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_API_KEY out of the main WarRoom frontend's own
#      .env — same Firebase project, so it works here verbatim.
#
# VERIFY a key before trusting it. This returns INVALID_LOGIN_CREDENTIALS when
# the key is good and API_KEY_INVALID when it is not — no real account needed:
#   curl -s -X POST "https://identitytoolkit.googleapis.com/v1/accounts:signInWithPassword?key=$KEY" \
#     -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
#     -d '{"email":"probe@example.invalid","password":"x","returnSecureToken":true}'
#
# This is a PUBLIC CLIENT IDENTIFIER, not a credential — it ships in every built
# bundle by design, and is kept out of git for tidiness rather than secrecy. It
# grants nothing on its own; Firebase Auth and Security Rules decide access. It
# is still worth restricting: console → APIs & Services → Credentials → that key
# → Application restrictions → HTTP referrers → your origins. (It currently has
# none, which is why it works from localhost unchanged. Check with:
#   gcloud services api-keys describe <UID> \
#     --project=warroom-498513 --format='yaml(restrictions)')
#
# ⚠ SIGN-IN IS DISABLED UNTIL THIS IS SET. appConfig.isConfigured() returns
#   false on an empty key and the login button does nothing — which looks
#   exactly like a broken login. If local sign-in is silent, check this first.
VITE_FIREBASE_API_KEY=

# WHAT:  The Firebase project. Must match the backend's FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID, or
#        the backend rejects every token this app mints with a 401.
# WHERE: Firebase console → Project settings → Project ID.
#        Or: gcloud config get-value project
#        Not a secret.
VITE_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=warroom-498513

# WHAT:  The sign-in domain the Firebase SDK talks to.
# WHERE: Defaults to "<project-id>.firebaseapp.com", which is correct here — it
#        is one of this project's two authorized domains. Set it explicitly only
#        if you configure a custom auth domain. To see the current list:
#          curl -s "https://identitytoolkit.googleapis.com/v1/projects?key=$KEY"
#
#        FYI: `localhost` is NOT in that list, and Firebase normally adds it by
#        default — somebody removed it. It does not affect you today, because
#        this app only uses signInWithEmailAndPassword, which ignores the
#        authorized-domain list. It WOULD break the moment anyone adds Google or
#        any other OAuth popup/redirect sign-in — and it would break on Vercel
#        before it broke locally, since humanfirst-city.vercel.app is not listed
#        either. Fix then via Firebase console → Authentication → Settings →
#        Authorized domains.
# VITE_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=warroom-498513.firebaseapp.com


# ── Backend ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# WHAT:  The academy backend's ORIGIN. Academy routes live at /api/v1 off the
#        root, so do NOT include a path — a trailing "/api" is stripped
#        automatically (the main WarRoom frontend's own convention ends in /api,
#        and that would double up to /api/api/v1).
# WHERE: local        http://localhost:8080      ← matches the backend's PORT=8080
#        deployed     gcloud run services describe warroom-academy-backend \
#                       --project=warroom-498513 --region=us-central1 \
#                       --format='value(status.url)'
#                     → https://warroom-academy-backend-262374983592.us-central1.run.app
#
# ⚠ Whichever origin you point at must list THIS app's origin in its
#   CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS, or every call fails in the browser with a CORS error
#   while the same URL succeeds under curl. For local dev that is
#   http://localhost:5173.
VITE_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8080

# WHAT:  Where a Firebase user with no WarRoom account is sent to sign up.
# WHERE: The main WarRoom site's register page. Should equal the backend's
#        MAIN_SITE_URL + "/register".
VITE_REGISTER_URL=https://warroom.humanfirstbykk.com/register


# ── Local development only ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

# WHAT:  Mount the city world WITHOUT signing in, so the map can be eyeballed
#        and screenshotted with no Firebase key and no backend running:
#            VITE_DEV_WORLD=1 npm run dev
# WHERE: Your choice. Safe to leave in .env: it is gated on import.meta.env.DEV,
#        which is statically false in a production build, so the branch is
#        dead-code-eliminated and can never ship. Anything backed by the API is
#        empty in this mode.
# VITE_DEV_WORLD=1


# ── Compatibility ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The main WarRoom frontend's NEXT_PUBLIC_* names are ALSO accepted, so its .env
# drops in verbatim. The canonical VITE_* name wins when both are present.
#
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_API_KEY      → VITE_FIREBASE_API_KEY
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID   → VITE_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN  → VITE_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL               → VITE_API_BASE_URL   (its trailing /api is stripped)
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_REGISTER_URL          → VITE_REGISTER_URL


# ── Deploying (Vercel) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# These are build-time values, so a change only takes effect on a REDEPLOY —
# editing them in the dashboard does nothing to the currently-served bundle.
#   Dashboard: Vercel → the humanfirst-city project → Settings → Environment
#              Variables → set for Production/Preview/Development → Redeploy
#   CLI:       vercel env add VITE_FIREBASE_API_KEY production
#              vercel env ls
# Set at minimum VITE_FIREBASE_API_KEY and VITE_API_BASE_URL (pointing at the
# Cloud Run URL, not localhost).
